1:# Proofs and counterexamples: repeated-design M-estimation
3:Date: 2026-10-14 (PT). These are complete mathematical arguments, not Lean formalizations. No priority claim is made. The original **weak-consistency problem for a nested deterministic design, with a well-behaved fixed selection or unique minimizers, remains unresolved here**. Theorems below must not be substituted for that problem.
5:## 1. Definitions and the three distinct experiments
7:Fix a finite dimension p, a deterministic infinite sequence x_i in R^p, a finite convex function rho:R -> R with rho(t) -> infinity as |t| -> infinity, a parameter beta_0, and i.i.d. errors. The loss does not change with n. Write
15:* **R: two fixed replicate streams.** Take independent i.i.d. sequences epsilon_i and epsilon_i'. The pooled criterion is F_n + F_n', each using x_1,...,x_n. Both streams are nested as n grows.
16:* **S: the book-indexed sliding second block.** On one i.i.d. sequence, use epsilon_1,...,epsilon_n for the original experiment and epsilon_{n+1},...,epsilon_{2n} at the repeated rows x_1,...,x_n. The second block is fresh relative to the first at each n, but is not a nested replicate stream across n.
17:* **T: triangular designs.** The design x_{n,i} is allowed to change at old indices as n changes. This is not a single nested sequence x_i.
21:## 2. Scalar preservation and least-squares preservation
23:### Theorem 1 (scalar, unique half minimizers)
33:### Theorem 2 (least squares, all dimensions)
39:## 3. Strong consistency fails for sliding blocks, even for scalar least squares
41:### Theorem 3
76:**Scope.** This does not contradict Theorem 2, or ordinary nested-design least-squares consistency theorems. The repeated 2n-row arrays in S are not initial segments of one fixed infinite design. Under R, the corresponding estimator is (A_j+A_j')/2 and is strongly consistent. Any statement of the strong analogue must name its coupling.
78:## 4. The Gram eigenvalue condition is not necessary for general convex M-estimation
80:### Theorem 4A (LAD with an atom: eventual exact recovery)
82:Take p=1, x_i=1/i, rho(r)=|r|, and probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4 at errors -1,0,+1. For every beta_0, almost surely the LAD minimizer equals beta_0 uniquely for all sufficiently large n. Nevertheless S_n=sum_{i<=n}i^{-2}<2 for every n.
91:### Theorem 4B (continuous bounded errors and a flat-bottom loss)
107:## 5. A genuine failure under triangular designs, with no arbitrary selection trick
109:### Theorem 5
111:There is a fixed finite coercive convex loss, bounded nondegenerate i.i.d. errors, p=4, and deterministic triangular designs for which **every original minimizing selection is strongly consistent**, but **every replicated minimizing selection is weakly inconsistent**. This is not a counterexample for a single nested design sequence.
123:This specifies ordinary deterministic design vectors in the beta coordinates. Couple errors by using two fixed high-row errors e_1,e_2 and six independent infinite Rademacher streams for the six groups. All errors within any stage are i.i.d. Use a second independent copy of these eight streams for the repeated experiment. Sizes between n_m and n_{m+1} may be defined by the stage-m design plus zero rows; their extra losses do not affect minimizers.
151:The strong law sends the two coefficients to 0 and c. There are only six groups and finitely many linear forms, so convergence is uniform on every compact set, almost surely. A countable sequence of balls makes this simultaneous on all compacts. The duplicate proof is identical. QED.
169:For completeness, local uniform convergence of convex functions to a coercive continuous function with a unique minimizer forces every minimizer to converge to that point. Choose a sphere centered at the limiting minimizer on which the limiting objective exceeds its minimum by a positive amount. Uniform convergence on that sphere and at its center gives the same strict inequality for all sufficiently large finite objectives. If one had a minimizer outside the sphere, convexity along the segment from the center to it would contradict the sphere inequality. Compactness then puts all minimizers in any prescribed neighborhood by uniform convergence and the uniqueness gap. In the present construction every finite objective has minimizers: its design has rank four, and rho is coercive, so its objective is coercive.
177:**Why this does not settle the nested problem.** The first two rows change with m, and the fourth components of low rows in groups 3, 4, and 6 change with m. One cannot reinterpret this array as prefixes of a fixed x_i sequence. Attempts to concatenate stages leave old weak-direction penalties in the objective and destroy the argument. This obstruction has not been overcome.
179:## 6. A literal arbitrary-selection version is false, but for an uninteresting reason
181:### Theorem 6 (selection diagnostic, not a resolution of the intended conjecture)
183:Use p=1, beta_0=0, x_i=2^{-i}, errors equal to -1/4 or +1/4 independently, and rho(r)=(|r|-1)_+. Define one fixed measurable rule on a finite scalar dataset as follows: among its minimizers, choose the point closest to zero if all nonzero design rows are distinct; otherwise choose the largest minimizer. For other datasets where a largest minimizer does not exist, use the closest-to-zero selection; this fallback is irrelevant here.
187:**Proof.** The minimizing interval is the intersection of [2^i(epsilon_i-1),2^i(epsilon_i+1)]. All these intervals contain [-3/2,3/2]. The first row's interval is contained in [-5/2,5/2], while all later rows' intervals contain [-3,3], so the first row determines the original interval. The original designs are distinct, hence the rule chooses zero. In the duplicated design there are two first-row intervals and all later intervals are wider; their intersection still contains [-3/2,3/2] and has a largest point at least 3/2. The fixed rule chooses that point. These choices are measurable (finite maxima, minima, and a deterministic test on the design). QED.
191:## 7. A necessary-information lemma for the actual nested problem
193:### Theorem 7 (regular location errors and equivariant estimation)
195:Suppose the errors have a density f on R, q=sqrt(f) belongs to the Sobolev space H^1(R), and J=4 integral |q'(x)|^2 dx is finite. Let x_i be one fixed deterministic infinite design. If a translation-equivariant estimator is weakly consistent at one parameter value, then lambda_min(S_n)->infinity. Translation equivariance means T_n(y+X_n h)=T_n(y)+h for every parameter shift h. Unique M-minimizers automatically have this property wherever they exist.
199:**Proof.** Equivariance makes the estimation-error distribution independent of beta_0, so consistency holds at every parameter value. Suppose lambda_min(S_n) does not diverge. Since S_n increases in the positive-semidefinite order, these smallest eigenvalues are bounded by a constant C. Choose unit eigenvectors along a subsequence converging to a unit vector u. For each fixed m and all later n, u_n'S_m u_n<=u_n'S_n u_n<=C. Passing to the subsequential limit gives u'S_m u<=C for every m. Therefore sum_i (x_i'u)^2<=C.
217:For Gaussian errors of variance sigma^2, J=1/sigma^2. The lemma also covers other finite-location-information densities satisfying the displayed H^1 condition; it does not cover atomic laws or the uniform-density support boundary in Theorems 4A/4B. It closes the *necessary design condition* for equivariant estimators in this regular class. Applying a suitable independently established M-consistency sufficiency theorem would then give weak transfer because duplication replaces S_n by 2S_n. A general sufficiency theorem for every finite convex coercive rho has not been established here, and the truncated 1993 preview is not used to assume one.
219:## 8. A direct necessary design condition for LAD, including atoms
221:### Theorem 8 (two-sided errors force L1 design divergence)
223:Let rho(r)=|r|, let span{x_i:i>=1}=R^p for a single nested deterministic design, and suppose the i.i.d. error law satisfies P(epsilon>0)>0 and P(epsilon<0)>0. If even one measurable LAD minimizing selection is weakly consistent at beta_0, then
229:**Proof.** Suppose ell_n does not diverge. It is nondecreasing, so ell_n<=C for every n. Choose unit minimizing directions along a subsequence converging to u. For each fixed m, continuity and nestedness give sum_{i<=m}|x_i'u|<=C. Thus L=sum_{i>=1}|x_i'u| is finite. It is positive because u is a unit vector and the design spans R^p. Choose M with A=sum_{i<=M}|x_i'u|>L/2; put B=L-A<A.
231:There is an r>0 with both P(epsilon>=r)>0 and P(epsilon<=-r)>0. Consider the event E on the first M errors requiring epsilon_i>=r when x_i'u>0, and epsilon_i<=-r when x_i'u<0; impose no condition when x_i'u=0. Independence and finiteness give P(E)>0. Choose delta>0 so that ||x_i|| delta<r/2 for every relevant i<=M.
233:On E, for every b with ||b-beta_0||<=delta, the first M residuals in the relevant rows have the prescribed nonzero signs. Their directional derivatives along +u therefore sum to -A. Every later absolute-loss term has right directional derivative at most |x_i'u|, including when its residual vanishes. Consequently, for every n>=M and every such b,
237:A point with a negative right directional derivative cannot minimize a finite convex function. Hence on E no minimizing selection lies within the closed delta-ball, for any n>=M. Its error probability is bounded below by P(E)>0, contradicting weak consistency. QED.
241:## 9. What has not been proved
243:No proof or non-selection counterexample is supplied for weak preservation under one fixed infinite deterministic design with p>=2 and unique original/pooled minimizers. Nor is the analogous strong R statement established in those dimensions outside the least-squares or other sufficient-condition regimes. The book additionally says that the error law satisfies unspecified conditions; those conditions must be made explicit before attributing any counterexample to the author's intended regularity class.

