    58	
    59	**Necessary conditions and literature gaps.** Bai–Wu (1997), [DOI 10.1016/S0167-7152(96)00182-4](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-7152(96)00182-4), and Berlinet–Liese–Vajda (2000), [DOI 10.1016/S0378-3758(99)00218-9](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0378-3758(99)00218-9), explicitly concern necessity or necessary/sufficient conditions. Their primary metadata was opened, but their complete theorem hypotheses were not accessible; no formula is guessed from their titles. Chen–Wu (1988), [DOI 10.1016/0047-259X(88)90120-0](https://doi.org/10.1016/0047-259X(88)90120-0), is similarly recorded without inventing its assumptions.
    60	
    61	**A proved reduction for the actual nested problem:** Theorem 7 shows that if sqrt(f) belongs to H^1 with finite location information, weak consistency of a translation-equivariant estimator forces lambda_min(S_n)->infinity. Unique M-minimizers are equivariant. Its product-affinity proof covers Gaussian errors and precisely explains why the atomic/bounded-support examples do not justify a universal necessity claim. A suitable M-estimation *sufficiency* theorem would finish the transfer in a matching regular class, since replication doubles S_n. It does not establish sufficiency for every convex rho.
    62	
    63	**A second necessary condition, including atomic LAD:** Theorem 8 proves directly that for a spanning fixed design and an error law with positive probability on both sides of zero, consistency of even one LAD minimizing selection requires inf_{||u||=1} sum_{i<=n}|x_i'u|->infinity. No density, moments, uniqueness, or equivariance is needed. A positive-probability finite-prefix event dominates any finite-L1 tail and excludes all minimizers near the truth. Replication doubles this quantity. Sufficiency is not claimed; the result is not attributed to the inaccessible Bai–Wu theorem.
    64	
    65	## Attack
    66	
    67	1. **Separate weak/strong, selection, and coupling first.** This produced the scalar bracket proof, the exact least-squares averaging identity, and a genuine strong S counterexample. The latter uses x_i=1 only at i=4^k and Rademacher errors. The original estimate averages the first j informative errors. Within each exponentially long interval of n with the same j, a greedy packing supplies many disjoint sliding windows; both all-plus and all-minus windows occur eventually in every interval. A summable probability bound proves the almost-sure oscillation. No simulation supplies this proof.
    68	2. **Test convexity plus exchangeability.** It is insufficient even for i.i.d. random convex functions. For delta>0, H_s=[[1,s delta],[s delta,delta^2+delta^4]], a_s=(s delta,0), and f_s(z)=(z-a_s)'H_s(z-a_s), each minimum tends to zero. For opposite signs, the sum's minimizer is (0,1/(1+delta^2)). These quadratics do not themselves have the required nested regression representation; they rule out a tempting abstract argument. Rational checks are in [evidence/exact-checks.json](evidence/exact-checks.json).
    69	3. **Realize the obstruction with one fixed regression loss.** Theorem 5 constructs six low-leverage hinge groups and two high rows, with rho(r)=10|r|+(r-1)_+ and Rademacher errors. It proves compact-uniform objective limits, coercivity, and convergence of all minimizers. The original fourth coefficient tends to zero; after replication it tends to -1,0,+1 with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4. This is a substantive triangular-design counterexample, not a nested one.
    70	4. **Attempt nesting rather than silently changing the target.** Freezing and concatenating stages retains old penalties. In the limiting old coordinates, G(0,0,0,v)=4v_+ + (-v)_+/2. Thus old stages penalize every nonzero weak-coordinate displacement; they cannot be discarded. Raising new-stage weights eventually breaks the bounded high-row pinning. Staggered high-row updates also need consistency at every intermediate prefix, not merely completed blocks. A one-sided-flat variant was explored to eliminate old penalties, but finite empirical groups retain random linear slopes in the unpenalized direction; no iid, fixed-loss construction controlling those slopes and preserving original consistency was obtained. These are failed constructions, **not impossibility theorems**.
    71	5. **Attack necessity in the genuine nested setting.** Theorem 7 extracts a fixed finite-energy direction if Gram divergence fails, then uses positive product Hellinger affinity to rule out consistency at two distinct parameters. Equivariance supplies the two-parameter requirement. Theorem 8 separately handles LAD with two-sided, possibly atomic errors by a finite-prefix directional-derivative obstruction. These give genuine necessary-condition reductions in the nested problem, but neither proves pooled consistency from necessity alone.
    72	6. **Check atoms/support rather than assuming LSE conditions.** Theorems 4A/4B give complete bounded-Gram examples, including eventual unique LAD recovery and shrinking nonunique intervals. Theorem 6 records why an arbitrary argmin selector makes the literal statement vacuous; it is not counted as settling the intended problem.
